1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,080
There was two hundred
thousand dollars in my trunk.

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:06,480
Cash, in a black duffel
bag that wasn't mine.

3
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:09,360
I was sitting on a gas
station curb at midnight,

4
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:12,800
just staring at it, and my
phone wouldn't stop ringing.

5
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:17,120
I'm Kai. I'm twenty-seven, I
drive for a rideshare app,

6
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:18,240
and until that night,

7
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:22,160
the weirdest thing anybody ever
left in my car was a live chicken.

8
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:23,840
Long story.

9
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:26,440
Okay, quick thing before I rewind.

10
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:28,040
This story splits.

11
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:30,800
At the end of this video,
you decide what I do,

12
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:33,000
and every choice leads
somewhere different.

13
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:34,480
There are four endings.

14
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:36,760
Only one of them tells
you the whole truth.

15
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:38,320
So keep your eyes open.

16
00:00:38,360 --> 00:00:40,720
I'm serious. The little stuff matters.

17
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:45,200
Three hours earlier, I was parked
outside a closed taco place,

18
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:48,840
eating a gas station sandwich
and staring at a text.

19
00:00:48,893 --> 00:00:52,000
"Your weekly rental payment
is three weeks past due.

20
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:55,200
Pay by Monday or the
vehicle will be recovered."

21
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:56,400
Recovered.

22
00:00:56,440 --> 00:00:57,840
Like it was lost.

23
00:00:57,880 --> 00:00:59,640
The car wasn't even mine.

24
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:02,480
I rented it through a program
for rideshare drivers,

25
00:01:02,520 --> 00:01:04,360
three hundred and fifty dollars a week,

26
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:07,267
because it was the only
way I could drive at all.

27
00:01:07,293 --> 00:01:10,240
And on my fridge back home,
there was another bill.

28
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:15,160
Eighteen thousand dollars, from the
night my appendix decided to quit on me.

29
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:16,440
No insurance.

30
00:01:16,467 --> 00:01:19,240
I'd been paying it off
fifty bucks at a time.

31
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:21,800
So that Friday, I had one plan.

32
00:01:21,880 --> 00:01:23,480
Drive until Monday.

33
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:25,800
Every ride, every tip.

34
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:27,520
Then the app pinged.

35
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:30,160
A pickup downtown, going to the airport.

36
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:31,213
Long ride.

37
00:01:31,267 --> 00:01:33,560
Good money. I took it.

38
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:37,200
He was waiting on the curb
outside an office building.

39
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:40,680
Older guy, maybe sixty,
in a wrinkled suit.

40
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:42,520
Before I could even get out to help,

41
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:46,240
he threw a black duffel bag in my trunk
and climbed in the back with a little

42
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:48,040
carry-on on his lap.

43
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:50,600
His name on the app was Harold.

44
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:54,600
The whole ride, he kept turning
around to look out the back window.

45
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:59,040
Halfway there, he asked me to skip the
highway and take the side streets.

46
00:01:59,080 --> 00:02:04,280
Then he said, kind of to himself,
"I canceled two cars before yours."

47
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:05,257
At a red light,

48
00:02:05,307 --> 00:02:08,880
he leaned forward and reached under the
seat in front of him for a good ten

49
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:10,080
seconds.

50
00:02:10,120 --> 00:02:12,280
"Dropped my pen," he said.

51
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:14,840
Right before the airport,
he asked for my number,

52
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:17,227
in case he left something in the car.

53
00:02:17,333 --> 00:02:19,360
People leave stuff all the time.

54
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:21,960
Nobody asks for your number first.

55
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,667
At departures, he grabbed my shoulder.

56
00:02:24,693 --> 00:02:26,600
"Thank you, Kai," he said.

57
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:28,040
"I'm sorry."

58
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:30,840
A traffic cop was already
waving me off the curb,

59
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:32,800
so I just drove.

60
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:37,240
I was halfway home when I hit the speed
bump at the all-night gas station by the

61
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:41,240
overpass, and something heavy
slammed around in my trunk.

62
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:44,080
I pulled up under the
lights and popped it open.

63
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:45,880
The black duffel bag.

64
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:47,360
Harold's bag.

65
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:51,760
It was heavy, way too heavy for
clothes, and the lining had this fresh,

66
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:52,960
crooked stitching,

67
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,600
like somebody had sewn it up in a
hurry with the wrong color thread.

68
00:02:56,680 --> 00:02:57,960
I unzipped it.

69
00:02:58,440 --> 00:03:01,200
Money. Stacks of hundred-dollar bills,

70
00:03:01,240 --> 00:03:05,000
every stack wrapped in a paper
band stamped with two letters.

71
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:06,680
B M.

72
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:10,080
I counted one stack with
shaking hands, did the math,

73
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:12,160
and had to sit down on the curb.

74
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:14,720
Two hundred thousand dollars.

75
00:03:14,760 --> 00:03:16,147
Enough for the hospital.

76
00:03:16,173 --> 00:03:18,227
The car. Everything.

77
00:03:18,253 --> 00:03:22,520
For about ten seconds, I just sat
there and let myself imagine it.

78
00:03:23,120 --> 00:03:24,720
Then my phone rang.

79
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:26,080
Unknown number.

80
00:03:26,240 --> 00:03:28,160
I almost let it go to voicemail.

81
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:29,320
"Is this Kai?

82
00:03:29,360 --> 00:03:30,440
The driver?"

83
00:03:30,480 --> 00:03:32,853
A woman, totally out of breath.

84
00:03:32,907 --> 00:03:34,440
"Do you have my dad's bag?

85
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:35,560
Harold Pruitt.

86
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:37,947
Please, I need you to listen to me."

87
00:03:37,973 --> 00:03:39,360
And I said yes.

88
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:40,987
I still don't know why.

89
00:03:41,013 --> 00:03:43,360
"Don't take it to the police," she said.

90
00:03:43,440 --> 00:03:46,760
"Not tonight. If that bag ends up
at the police station tonight,

91
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:48,560
my dad goes to prison.

92
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:51,040
I can explain everything, I swear.

93
00:03:51,120 --> 00:03:52,347
Just meet me.

94
00:03:52,693 --> 00:03:54,267
Please." Then she hung up,

95
00:03:54,293 --> 00:03:57,800
and a second later she texted
me the address of some diner.

96
00:03:57,840 --> 00:04:02,280
So there I was, on a curb, with a
bag full of somebody else's money,

97
00:04:02,320 --> 00:04:04,920
a stranger who'd just
been crying on the phone,

98
00:04:04,960 --> 00:04:07,240
and a rental payment I couldn't make.

99
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:09,640
You'd think the right
thing would be obvious.

100
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:10,760
It wasn't.

101
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:14,320
And whatever I was going
to do, I had to do it now.

102
00:04:14,920 --> 00:04:17,480
So yeah. That was my night so far.

103
00:04:17,560 --> 00:04:22,400
A bag of cash, a crying stranger,
and a diner address on my phone.

104
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:24,560
If you think I should meet
the woman who called,

105
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:26,400
tap the video on the left.

106
00:04:26,440 --> 00:04:29,960
If I should take the bag to the
police, tap the one on the right.

107
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,840
And you can always come back
and try the other path.
